  "answer": "Refund processing-state changes are applied in one function, `applyProcessingState` in `src/refund-settlement/status.ts:9-20`. It's the entry point. A grep of `src/refund-settlement` finds no callers there, so I can't say what calls it. I did not search outside that directory.\n\n**Signature**\n```ts\napplyProcessingState(\n  record: RecordState,          // { refundId: string; processingState: ProcessingState }\n  state: ProcessingState,       // \"queued\" | \"processing\" | \"settled\" | \"rejected\"\n  publish: (frame: object) => void,\n): void\n```\n\n**What it does**\n1. It mutates the record in place: `record.processingState = state` (line 14). There is no transition validation, so any state can follow any other.\n2. It then calls the injected `publish` callback once with a frame. The doc comment says these updates go to the operations dashboard, but this file doesn't show which transport `publish` uses.\n\n**Published frame**\n```ts\n{\n  resource: { id: record.refundId, processing_status: record.processingState },\n  type: \"refund_settlement\",\n  event: \"status_changed\",\n}\n```\n- `type` is `\"refund_settlement\"`.\n- `event` is `\"status_changed\"`.\n- `resource.id` is the refund ID.\n- `resource.processing_status` is the new state, read back from the record after the update.\n- The frame doesn't include the previous state or a timestamp.\n- The record field is `processingState` (camelCase), but the frame field is `processing_status` (snake_case). Dashboard consumers need the frame name.\n\n`src/marketing/status.ts` and `src/document-ingestion/status.ts` each define their own function with the same name and the same line range (9-20). They use a different `ProcessingState` union. Make sure you're looking at the refund-settlement one.",
